window.docContents[220].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Representation-of-a-quiver/#Chapter2___Definition2___8___3___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.8. Quivers\u0009Representation of a quiver\u0009Representation of a quiver","header":"Formalization","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Representation-of-a-quiver/#Chapter2___Definition2___8___3___formalization"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Concrete-Lie-algebra-examples-_LPAR_continued_RPAR____-aff_LPAR_1_RPAR____-so_LPAR_n_RPAR_/#Chapter2___Discussion_concrete_Lie_examples_continued":{"contents":"(4) The algebra \\operatorname{aff}(1) of matrices \\begin{pmatrix} * & * \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\nIts basis consists of X = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} and Y = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}, with [X, Y] = Y.\n\n(5) \\mathfrak{so}(n), the space of skew-symmetric n \\times n matrices, with [a, b] = ab - ba.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.9. Lie algebras\u0009Concrete Lie algebra examples (continued): aff(1), so(n)","header":"Concrete Lie algebra examples (continued): aff(1), so(n)","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Concrete-Lie-algebra-examples-_LPAR_continued_RPAR____-aff_LPAR_1_RPAR____-so_LPAR_n_RPAR_/#Chapter2___Discussion_concrete_Lie_examples_continued"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___1___-Maschke___s-theorem/#chapter-04___section-4-1":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results","header":"4.1. Maschke's theorem","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___1___-Maschke___s-theorem/#chapter-04___section-4-1"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/2-dimensional-irreducible-representation-of-Q_8-via-functions/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___3___-Examples--2-dimensional-irreducible-representation-of-Q_8-via-functions--2-dimensional-irreducible-representation-of-Q_8-via-functions--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"The auxiliary function submodule is preserved by the displayed group representation.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Exercise4.3.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe auxiliary complex function submodule has dimension two.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Exercise4.3.1; role=supporting]\n\nAn invariant submodule contained in the auxiliary function submodule is either zero or the whole auxiliary submodule.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Exercise4.3.1; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.3. Examples\u00092-dimensional irreducible representation of Q_8 via functions\u00092-dimensional irreducible representation of Q_8 via functions\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/2-dimensional-irreducible-representation-of-Q_8-via-functions/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___3___-Examples--2-dimensional-irreducible-representation-of-Q_8-via-functions--2-dimensional-irreducible-representation-of-Q_8-via-functions--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Algebraic-_LPAR_rational___-polynomial_RPAR_-representation-of-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____--Algebraic-_LPAR_rational___-polynomial_RPAR_-representation-of-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_--Algebraic-_LPAR_rational___-polynomial_RPAR_-representation-of-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary property of maps from a general linear group to linear endomorphisms.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Definition5.23.1; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary property of finite-dimensional representations of a general linear group.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Definition5.23.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe auxiliary representation property is equivalent to the auxiliary map property for its underlying function.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Definition5.23.1; role=supporting]\n\nThere exists a map with the auxiliary property whose value at the identity is not the identity linear map.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Definition5.23.1; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.23. Algebraic representations of $GL(V)$\u0009Algebraic (rational, polynomial) representation of GL(V)\u0009Algebraic (rational, polynomial) representation of GL(V)\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Algebraic-_LPAR_rational___-polynomial_RPAR_-representation-of-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____--Algebraic-_LPAR_rational___-polynomial_RPAR_-representation-of-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_--Algebraic-_LPAR_rational___-polynomial_RPAR_-representation-of-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___1___-Problems/Theorem___-Classification-of-Dynkin-diagrams/#Chapter6___Theorem_Dynkin_classification___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.1. Problems\u0009Theorem: Classification of Dynkin diagrams\u0009Theorem: Classification of Dynkin diagrams","header":"Formalization","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___1___-Problems/Theorem___-Classification-of-Dynkin-diagrams/#Chapter6___Theorem_Dynkin_classification___formalization"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___/Section-6___2-heading-and-introduction/#Chapter6___Section6___2_heading":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.2. Indecomposable representations of the quivers $A_1$, $A_2$, $A_3$\u0009Section 6.2 heading and introduction","header":"Section 6.2 heading and introduction","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___/Section-6___2-heading-and-introduction/#Chapter6___Section6___2_heading"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___5___-Representable-functors/Yoneda-Lemma/#Chapter7___Lemma7___5___1":{"contents":"Lemma 7.5.1 (The Yoneda Lemma). If a functor F is represented by an object X, then X is unique up to a unique isomorphism. I.e., if X, Y are two objects in \\mathcal{C}, then for any isomorphism of functors\n\\phi : \\operatorname{Hom}(X, ?) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(Y, ?) there is a unique isomorphism a_\\phi : X \\to Y inducing \\phi.\n\nProof (Sketch). One sets a_\\phi = \\phi_Y^{-1}(1_Y) and shows that it is invertible by constructing the inverse, which is a_\\phi^{-1} = \\phi_X(1_X). It remains to show that the composition both ways is the identity, which we will omit here. This establishes the existence of a_\\phi. Its uniqueness is verified in a straightforward manner. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.5. Representable functors\u0009Yoneda Lemma","header":"Yoneda Lemma","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___5___-Representable-functors/Yoneda-Lemma/#Chapter7___Lemma7___5___1"}});